linkrd for Debian
==================

linkrd synchronizes the plain-text clipboard of a desktop user session with a
Radxa Linkr device over USB CDC-ACM.  It must run as the desktop user whose
clipboard it synchronizes, so the Debian package ships a systemd *user* unit
instead of a system service.  The package installs, but deliberately does not
enable or start, that unit.

Installed files
---------------

  /usr/bin/linkrd
      The agent binary.
  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-linkrd.rules
      Grants the logged-in desktop user read/write access to the Linkr device
      (USB VID 1D6B, PID 0104 -> /dev/ttyACM*).
  /usr/lib/systemd/user/linkrd.service
      Per-user unit; starts with the graphical session once it is enabled.
  /usr/share/man/man1/linkrd.1.gz
  /usr/share/doc/linkrd/


Enabling the user service
-------------------------

Log in as the desktop user (do not use sudo) and run:

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now linkrd.service

Check it and follow its log:

  systemctl --user status linkrd.service
  journalctl --user -u linkrd.service -e
  linkrd status
  linkrd pid

The unit uses ExecStart=/usr/bin/linkrd with the built-in defaults (automatic
USB VID/PID selection, log level "info", log file in ~/.local/share/linkrd).
If you need different arguments, do not edit the unit file in /usr/lib: use a
drop-in instead, for example

  systemctl --user edit linkrd.service

and add

  [Service]
  ExecStart=
  ExecStart=/usr/bin/linkrd --port /dev/ttyACM0 --log-level debug

The service is bound to graphical-session.target (After= and PartOf=), so it
stops with the graphical session and starts again at the next login.  It is
not wanted by default.target on purpose: clipboard access requires a running
desktop session.

After a package upgrade, re-run "systemctl --user daemon-reload" and, if the
unit changed, "systemctl --user restart linkrd.service".  Upgrading does not
interrupt the running agent otherwise.


Do not use "linkrd install" with this package
---------------------------------------------

This package puts the binary in /usr/bin and relies on systemd.  Do not run
"linkrd install", "linkrd install --autostart", or "linkrd autostart enable"
on a system where this package is installed.  Those commands implement a
separate per-user installation mode:

  * "linkrd install" copies the running executable to ~/.local/bin/linkrd
    and writes ~/.config/linkrd/installation.json, which records the copy's
    path and SHA-256 checksum.
  * "linkrd install --autostart" additionally writes
    ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop, whose Exec line runs the *copy* with
    --startup.  --startup loads the saved arguments from installation.json and
    refuses to run if the executable does not match the recorded checksum.

A package upgrade replaces /usr/bin/linkrd only.  A private copy created by
"linkrd install" is never updated, and the stale copy keeps being started by
the XDG autostart entry at every login.  This can result in the old version
running next to the packaged one, or in a broken --startup entry.  Both modes
also contend for the same ~/.config/linkrd/runtime.lock, so only one instance
can run at a time.


Migrating from a manual installation to this package
----------------------------------------------------

If linkrd was previously installed per user with "linkrd install":

  # 1. Stop the manually started instance (run as the desktop user).
  linkrd stop
  ~/.local/bin/linkrd stop   # if "linkrd" in PATH is not the installed copy

  # 2. Remove the per-user installation.  This deletes ~/.local/bin/linkrd,
  #    ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop, the PATH block in the shell
  #    profile, and ~/.config/linkrd/installation.json.  Logs are kept.
  linkrd uninstall
  ~/.local/bin/linkrd uninstall   # if needed as above

  # 3. Make sure the per-user binary and autostart entry are gone.
  command -v linkrd                       # must print /usr/bin/linkrd
  ls ~/.local/bin/linkrd                  # must not exist
  ls ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop   # must not exist

  # 4. Enable the packaged user unit as described above.
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now linkrd.service

If "linkrd uninstall" refuses to remove an executable or autostart entry that
was modified outside linkrd, remove that file manually and retry.  The
~/.config/linkrd directory may still contain runtime lock files; it is safe to
keep it.  On systems where the package is removed before the manual
installation, run step 2 from a backup copy of the per-user binary, or delete
~/.local/bin/linkrd, ~/.config/autostart/linkrd.desktop, the PATH block, and
~/.config/linkrd/installation.json by hand.


Device access (udev)
--------------------

The udev rule matches the Linkr CDC-ACM device by USB IDs and grants access to
the user logged in on the local seat:

  SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d6b", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0104",
  TAG+="uaccess", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"

TAG+="uaccess" uses the systemd-logind seat ACL, so normally no group
membership is needed.  On systems without systemd-logind (or without seat ACL
support) the rule falls back to group dialout with mode 0660; if your user
cannot open /dev/ttyACM*, add the desktop user to the dialout group, then log
out and back in:

  sudo adduser "$USER" dialout

After installing or upgrading the package the udev rules are reloaded
automatically when udevadm is available (the prebuilt .deb does this in its
postinst).  To do it by hand:

  sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
  sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=tty --action=change

Check that the device is visible:

  linkrd --list-ports


Logs and configuration
----------------------

  ~/.config/linkrd/                  per-user configuration and runtime state
      installation.json              only present after a manual "linkrd install"
      runtime.lock, runtime.pid,     created while linkrd is running
      runtime.control.json, runtime.stop
      *.lock                         management/state locks
  ~/.local/share/linkrd/logs/        rolling logs (XDG_DATA_HOME is honoured)
      linkrd.log                     active log file
      linkrd.log.1 ... linkrd.log.6  rotated files

Log files are rotated daily and when a file would exceed 10 MiB; at most 7
files are kept.  The location can be changed with --log-dir, or file logging
can be disabled with --no-file-log.  With systemd, stderr output also goes to
the user journal ("journalctl --user -u linkrd.service").  NO_COLOR is honoured
for stderr output.


Uninstalling
------------

  systemctl --user disable --now linkrd.service
  sudo apt remove linkrd

Removing the package does not touch the user's logs or configuration.  Remove
them manually if desired:

  rm -rf ~/.local/share/linkrd ~/.config/linkrd

If a manual per-user installation still exists, uninstall it first as
described above; otherwise linkrd may keep running from ~/.local/bin.


Source package
--------------

This package is built from the upstream git tree; see debian/README.source and
packaging/deb/README.md for details.  The prebuilt binary packages distributed
next to this source tree are statically linked against musl and have no libc
dependency; a build from this source package is linked against the distribution
glibc and gets its library dependencies through ${shlibs:Depends}.
